<html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Unit 5 diagram gallery</title><style>body{font:18px system-ui;max-width:1200px;margin:30px auto;color:#18354e}section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px}img{width:100%}article{border:1px solid #ccdbe5;padding:12px}h2{margin-top:55px}</style><h1>Unit 5 tabs and accordion diagrams</h1><h2>Lesson 1, section 1: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>00-00 Transverse Waves</h3><img src="images/00-00-transverse-waves.png"><p>Transverse Waves. Particle motion is perpendicular to wave travel.</p></article><article><h3>00-01 Longitudinal Waves</h3><img src="images/00-01-longitudinal-waves.png"><p>Longitudinal Waves. Particles oscillate parallel to the direction of travel.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>01-00 Crest</h3><img src="images/01-00-crest.png"><p>Crest. Crest: the highest point above equilibrium.</p></article><article><h3>01-01 Trough</h3><img src="images/01-01-trough.png"><p>Trough. Trough: the lowest point below equilibrium.</p></article><article><h3>01-02 Amplitude</h3><img src="images/01-02-amplitude.png"><p>Amplitude. Amplitude is not the crest-to-trough height.</p></article><article><h3>01-03 Wavelength (λ)</h3><img src="images/01-03-wavelength.png"><p>Wavelength (λ). Measure between matching points in consecutive cycles.</p></article><article><h3>01-04 Equilibrium position</h3><img src="images/01-04-equilibrium-position.png"><p>Equilibrium position. The center line represents the undisturbed position.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 2: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>02-00 Frequency</h3><img src="images/02-00-frequency.png"><p>Frequency. Count cycles per second for frequency; measure seconds per cycle for period.</p></article><article><h3>02-01 Period</h3><img src="images/02-01-period.png"><p>Period. Count cycles per second for frequency; measure seconds per cycle for period.</p></article><article><h3>02-02 Relationship</h3><img src="images/02-02-relationship.png"><p>Relationship. Count cycles per second for frequency; measure seconds per cycle for period.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>03-00 Wave Speed (v)</h3><img src="images/03-00-wave-speed-v.png"><p>Wave Speed (v). Wave speed describes travel of the disturbance, not the vibration speed of a particle.</p></article><article><h3>03-01 Wavelength (λ)</h3><img src="images/03-01-wavelength.png"><p>Wavelength (λ). Measure between matching points in consecutive cycles.</p></article><article><h3>03-02 Frequency (f)</h3><img src="images/03-02-frequency-f.png"><p>Frequency (f). Wave speed describes travel of the disturbance, not the vibration speed of a particle.</p></article><article><h3>03-03 The Equation</h3><img src="images/03-03-the-equation.png"><p>The Equation. Wave speed describes travel of the disturbance, not the vibration speed of a particle.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 2: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>04-00 Frequency Increases</h3><img src="images/04-00-frequency-increases.png"><p>Frequency Increases. For this nondispersive model, frequency and wavelength change inversely.</p></article><article><h3>04-01 Frequency Decreases</h3><img src="images/04-01-frequency-decreases.png"><p>Frequency Decreases. For this nondispersive model, frequency and wavelength change inversely.</p></article><article><h3>04-02 Visual Prediction</h3><img src="images/04-02-visual-prediction.png"><p>Visual Prediction. For this nondispersive model, frequency and wavelength change inversely.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 3: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>05-00 Medium Properties</h3><img src="images/05-00-medium-properties.png"><p>Medium Properties. The source sets frequency; the medium determines wave speed in this model.</p></article><article><h3>05-01 Frequency and Wavelength</h3><img src="images/05-01-frequency-and-wavelength.png"><p>Frequency and Wavelength. For this nondispersive model, frequency and wavelength change inversely.</p></article><article><h3>05-02 Crossing a Boundary</h3><img src="images/05-02-crossing-a-boundary.png"><p>Crossing a Boundary. The source sets frequency; the medium determines wave speed in this model.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>06-00 Reflection</h3><img src="images/06-00-reflection.png"><p>Reflection. A transverse pulse reflecting at a fixed end is inverted.</p></article><article><h3>06-01 Transmission</h3><img src="images/06-01-transmission.png"><p>Transmission. A transmitted wave enters the second medium; its speed and wavelength may change.</p></article><article><h3>06-02 Partial Reflection/Transmission</h3><img src="images/06-02-partial-reflection-transmission.png"><p>Partial Reflection/Transmission. At a transparent boundary, some energy can reflect and some can transmit.</p></article><article><h3>06-03 Refraction</h3><img src="images/06-03-refraction.png"><p>Refraction. Light entering glass obliquely bends toward the normal because its speed decreases.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 3: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>07-00 Constructive Interference</h3><img src="images/07-00-constructive-interference.png"><p>Constructive Interference. Displacements add while the waves overlap.</p></article><article><h3>07-01 Destructive Interference</h3><img src="images/07-01-destructive-interference.png"><p>Destructive Interference. Equal opposite displacements cancel locally; the waves are not destroyed.</p></article><article><h3>07-02 Superposition Principle</h3><img src="images/07-02-superposition-principle.png"><p>Superposition Principle. In a linear medium, pulses pass through one another and continue along their paths.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 1, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>08-00 Definition</h3><img src="images/08-00-definition.png"><p>Definition. Wavefronts spread into the region beyond an opening.</p></article><article><h3>08-01 Small Opening (relative to wavelength)</h3><img src="images/08-01-small-opening-relative-to-wavelength.png"><p>Small Opening (relative to wavelength). An opening comparable to wavelength produces substantial spreading.</p></article><article><h3>08-02 Large Opening (relative to wavelength)</h3><img src="images/08-02-large-opening-relative-to-wavelength.png"><p>Large Opening (relative to wavelength). A much wider opening produces a narrower central spread.</p></article><article><h3>08-03 Examples</h3><img src="images/08-03-examples.png"><p>Examples. Sound spreading through a doorway can reach a listener off to the side.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>09-00 How Do Particles Move?</h3><img src="images/09-00-how-do-particles-move.png"><p>How Do Particles Move?. A longitudinal sound wave moves through the medium while particles vibrate about equilibrium.</p></article><article><h3>09-01 Compressions and Rarefactions</h3><img src="images/09-01-compressions-and-rarefactions.png"><p>Compressions and Rarefactions. A longitudinal sound wave moves through the medium while particles vibrate about equilibrium.</p></article><article><h3>09-02 Tracking a Particle</h3><img src="images/09-02-tracking-a-particle.png"><p>Tracking a Particle. A longitudinal sound wave moves through the medium while particles vibrate about equilibrium.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>10-00 Frequency Controls Pitch</h3><img src="images/10-00-frequency-controls-pitch.png"><p>Frequency Controls Pitch. More cycles per second correspond to higher pitch.</p></article><article><h3>10-01 Amplitude Controls Loudness</h3><img src="images/10-01-amplitude-controls-loudness.png"><p>Amplitude Controls Loudness. At the same frequency, larger sound-pressure amplitude usually sounds louder.</p></article><article><h3>10-02 Pitch and Loudness Are Independent</h3><img src="images/10-02-pitch-and-loudness-are-independent.png"><p>Pitch and Loudness Are Independent. Frequency and amplitude can be adjusted independently.</p></article><article><h3>10-03 Frequency Ranges</h3><img src="images/10-03-frequency-ranges.png"><p>Frequency Ranges. The frequency scale is schematic, not linear.</p></article><article><h3>10-04 Decibel Scale</h3><img src="images/10-04-decibel-scale.png"><p>Decibel Scale. Each 10 dB increase means 10 times the intensity, not 10 times perceived loudness.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>11-00 Natural Frequency and Resonance</h3><img src="images/11-00-natural-frequency-and-resonance.png"><p>Natural Frequency and Resonance. Resonance transfers energy effectively when a periodic drive matches a natural mode.</p></article><article><h3>11-01 Standing Waves</h3><img src="images/11-01-standing-waves.png"><p>Standing Waves. These curves represent particle displacement, not pressure.</p></article><article><h3>11-02 Nodes and Antinodes</h3><img src="images/11-02-nodes-and-antinodes.png"><p>Nodes and Antinodes. These curves represent particle displacement, not pressure.</p></article><article><h3>11-03 Open and Closed Pipes</h3><img src="images/11-03-open-and-closed-pipes.png"><p>Open and Closed Pipes. Displacement patterns: open ends are antinodes; a closed end is a node.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>12-00 Source Moving Toward Observer</h3><img src="images/12-00-source-moving-toward-observer.png"><p>Source Moving Toward Observer. Approaching source: closer wavefront spacing and higher observed frequency.</p></article><article><h3>12-01 Source Moving Away from Observer</h3><img src="images/12-01-source-moving-away-from-observer.png"><p>Source Moving Away from Observer. Receding source: wider spacing and lower observed frequency.</p></article><article><h3>12-02 Stationary Source or Observer</h3><img src="images/12-02-stationary-source-or-observer.png"><p>Stationary Source or Observer. No relative motion through the medium gives no classical Doppler shift.</p></article><article><h3>12-03 Wave Speed Stays the Same</h3><img src="images/12-03-wave-speed-stays-the-same.png"><p>Wave Speed Stays the Same. Wavefronts still travel at the sound speed relative to the medium.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 3: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>13-00 Ahead of Source</h3><img src="images/13-00-ahead-of-source.png"><p>Ahead of Source. Ahead: compressed wavefront spacing.</p></article><article><h3>13-01 Behind Source</h3><img src="images/13-01-behind-source.png"><p>Behind Source. Behind: expanded wavefront spacing.</p></article><article><h3>13-02 Beside Source</h3><img src="images/13-02-beside-source.png"><p>Beside Source. In the classical model, transverse emission has zero radial source velocity.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 2, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>14-00 Wavefront Buildup</h3><img src="images/14-00-wavefront-buildup.png"><p>Wavefront Buildup. The idealized Mach 1 limit has wavefronts piling up at the forward edge.</p></article><article><h3>14-01 Mach 1 and the Mach Cone</h3><img src="images/14-01-mach-1-and-the-mach-cone.png"><p>Mach 1 and the Mach Cone. A finite Mach cone forms for supersonic motion; Mach 1 is the limiting case.</p></article><article><h3>14-02 Shock Wave and Sonic Boom</h3><img src="images/14-02-shock-wave-and-sonic-boom.png"><p>Shock Wave and Sonic Boom. The rapid pressure change at a passing shock is heard as a sonic boom.</p></article><article><h3>14-03 Not Just Once!</h3><img src="images/14-03-not-just-once.png"><p>Not Just Once!. The source continues producing a shock along its supersonic flight path.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 3, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>15-00 Radio Waves</h3><img src="images/15-00-radio-waves.png"><p>Radio Waves. Radio and microwaves overlap in naming: microwaves are a subset of radio waves.</p></article><article><h3>15-01 Microwaves</h3><img src="images/15-01-microwaves.png"><p>Microwaves. Microwaves support radar, communication, and energy transfer to matter.</p></article><article><h3>15-02 Infrared</h3><img src="images/15-02-infrared.png"><p>Infrared. Infrared radiation can carry energy from warm objects to a detector.</p></article><article><h3>15-03 Visible Light</h3><img src="images/15-03-visible-light.png"><p>Visible Light. Visible light is the portion detected by human vision.</p></article><article><h3>15-04 Ultraviolet</h3><img src="images/15-04-ultraviolet.png"><p>Ultraviolet. UV photons interact with matter differently from visible photons.</p></article><article><h3>15-05 X-rays</h3><img src="images/15-05-x-rays.png"><p>X-rays. X-ray images use differences in attenuation through materials.</p></article><article><h3>15-06 Gamma Rays</h3><img src="images/15-06-gamma-rays.png"><p>Gamma Rays. Gamma rays can be emitted when an excited nucleus loses energy.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 3, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>16-00 Refraction</h3><img src="images/16-00-refraction.png"><p>Refraction. Refraction depends on refractive index and angle of incidence, not mass density alone.</p></article><article><h3>16-01 Refractive Index</h3><img src="images/16-01-refractive-index.png"><p>Refractive Index. A larger refractive index means a lower light speed at the specified wavelength.</p></article><article><h3>16-02 Speed of Light in Different Materials</h3><img src="images/16-02-speed-of-light-in-different-materials.png"><p>Speed of Light in Different Materials. Illustrative speeds; refractive index varies with wavelength and material.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 3, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>17-00 Red</h3><img src="images/17-00-red.png"><p>Red. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article><article><h3>17-01 Orange</h3><img src="images/17-01-orange.png"><p>Orange. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article><article><h3>17-02 Yellow</h3><img src="images/17-02-yellow.png"><p>Yellow. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article><article><h3>17-03 Green</h3><img src="images/17-03-green.png"><p>Green. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article><article><h3>17-04 Blue</h3><img src="images/17-04-blue.png"><p>Blue. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article><article><h3>17-05 Violet</h3><img src="images/17-05-violet.png"><p>Violet. Illustrative wavelength within the color band; color boundaries are approximate.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>18-00 Step 1: Encoding Digital Information</h3><img src="images/18-00-step-1-encoding-digital-information.png"><p>Step 1: Encoding Digital Information. One simplified modulation scheme maps bits to different carrier amplitudes.</p></article><article><h3>18-01 Step 2: Transmitting the Signal</h3><img src="images/18-01-step-2-transmitting-the-signal.png"><p>Step 2: Transmitting the Signal. A transmitting antenna radiates an electromagnetic signal.</p></article><article><h3>18-02 Step 3: Traveling Through Space</h3><img src="images/18-02-step-3-traveling-through-space.png"><p>Step 3: Traveling Through Space. Electromagnetic waves propagate through air and vacuum.</p></article><article><h3>18-03 Step 4: Receiving the Signal</h3><img src="images/18-03-step-4-receiving-the-signal.png"><p>Step 4: Receiving the Signal. A receiving antenna responds to the arriving electromagnetic field.</p></article><article><h3>18-04 Step 5: Decoding the Information</h3><img src="images/18-04-step-5-decoding-the-information.png"><p>Step 5: Decoding the Information. The receiver recovers the bit pattern from the modulated signal.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 1: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>19-00 Short Wavelength, High Frequency</h3><img src="images/19-00-short-wavelength-high-frequency.png"><p>Short Wavelength, High Frequency. Shorter wavelength means higher carrier frequency; bandwidth and noise govern data capacity.</p></article><article><h3>19-01 Long Wavelength, Low Frequency</h3><img src="images/19-01-long-wavelength-low-frequency.png"><p>Long Wavelength, Low Frequency. Shorter wavelength means higher carrier frequency; bandwidth and noise govern data capacity.</p></article><article><h3>19-02 Choosing the Right Wave</h3><img src="images/19-02-choosing-the-right-wave.png"><p>Choosing the Right Wave. Carrier frequency alone does not set data rate or range.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>20-00 Distance from Transmitter</h3><img src="images/20-00-distance-from-transmitter.png"><p>Distance from Transmitter. Greater distance usually reduces received signal strength.</p></article><article><h3>20-01 Obstacles</h3><img src="images/20-01-obstacles.png"><p>Obstacles. Materials can absorb and reflect a signal.</p></article><article><h3>20-02 Interference</h3><img src="images/20-02-interference.png"><p>Interference. Competing signals can reduce the receiver’s ability to recover data.</p></article><article><h3>20-03 Weather and Atmosphere</h3><img src="images/20-03-weather-and-atmosphere.png"><p>Weather and Atmosphere. Rain attenuation is important in some high-frequency communication bands.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 2: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>21-00 Bluetooth</h3><img src="images/21-00-bluetooth.png"><p>Bluetooth. Personal devices exchange data over a nearby wireless link.</p></article><article><h3>21-01 Wi-Fi</h3><img src="images/21-01-wi-fi.png"><p>Wi-Fi. Devices communicate with a local access point.</p></article><article><h3>21-02 Cellular</h3><img src="images/21-02-cellular.png"><p>Cellular. Cellular networks transfer a moving connection between coverage areas.</p></article><article><h3>21-03 Satellite</h3><img src="images/21-03-satellite.png"><p>Satellite. Signal travel time depends on the total path length.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>22-00 Frequency and Wavelength</h3><img src="images/22-00-frequency-and-wavelength.png"><p>Frequency and Wavelength. Shorter wavelength means higher carrier frequency; bandwidth and noise govern data capacity.</p></article><article><h3>22-01 Power of the Transmitter</h3><img src="images/22-01-power-of-the-transmitter.png"><p>Power of the Transmitter. More power can improve the received signal, at an energy cost.</p></article><article><h3>22-02 Antenna Design</h3><img src="images/22-02-antenna-design.png"><p>Antenna Design. Antenna direction and gain affect how energy is distributed.</p></article><article><h3>22-03 Obstacles and Interference</h3><img src="images/22-03-obstacles-and-interference.png"><p>Obstacles and Interference. Obstacles and competing signals reduce link quality in different ways.</p></article><article><h3>22-04 Atmosphere and Weather</h3><img src="images/22-04-atmosphere-and-weather.png"><p>Atmosphere and Weather. Weather effects depend on the frequency band and path.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 3: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>23-00 Radio Waves</h3><img src="images/23-00-radio-waves.png"><p>Radio Waves. Radio and microwaves overlap in naming: microwaves are a subset of radio waves.</p></article><article><h3>23-01 Microwaves</h3><img src="images/23-01-microwaves.png"><p>Microwaves. Microwaves support radar, communication, and energy transfer to matter.</p></article><article><h3>23-02 Infrared</h3><img src="images/23-02-infrared.png"><p>Infrared. Infrared radiation can carry energy from warm objects to a detector.</p></article><article><h3>23-03 Visible Light</h3><img src="images/23-03-visible-light.png"><p>Visible Light. Visible light is the portion detected by human vision.</p></article><article><h3>23-04 Ultraviolet</h3><img src="images/23-04-ultraviolet.png"><p>Ultraviolet. UV photons interact with matter differently from visible photons.</p></article><article><h3>23-05 X-rays</h3><img src="images/23-05-x-rays.png"><p>X-rays. X-ray images use differences in attenuation through materials.</p></article><article><h3>23-06 Gamma Rays</h3><img src="images/23-06-gamma-rays.png"><p>Gamma Rays. Gamma rays can be emitted when an excited nucleus loses energy.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 4, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>24-00 Frequency &amp; Wavelength</h3><img src="images/24-00-frequency-wavelength.png"><p>Frequency &amp; Wavelength. Penetration is material- and frequency-dependent; no band passes through everything.</p></article><article><h3>24-01 Energy &amp; Interactions</h3><img src="images/24-01-energy-interactions.png"><p>Energy &amp; Interactions. Photon energy rises with frequency; ionization depends on the photon and target.</p></article><article><h3>24-02 Real-World Choices</h3><img src="images/24-02-real-world-choices.png"><p>Real-World Choices. Choose the spectral band for the physical interaction or information required.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 1: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>25-00 Increasing Frequency</h3><img src="images/25-00-increasing-frequency.png"><p>Increasing Frequency. E = hf: higher frequency means more energy per photon. Threshold depends on the material.</p></article><article><h3>25-01 Increasing Intensity</h3><img src="images/25-01-increasing-intensity.png"><p>Increasing Intensity. More photons per second can eject more electrons; maximum electron energy stays the same.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 1: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>26-00 Doppler Shift</h3><img src="images/26-00-doppler-shift.png"><p>Doppler Shift. A blueshift moves spectral lines to shorter wavelengths; a redshift moves them to longer wavelengths.</p></article><article><h3>26-01 Color &amp; Mixing</h3><img src="images/26-01-color-mixing.png"><p>Color &amp; Mixing. Adding light combines signals; pigments and filters remove parts of the incident spectrum.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 1: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>27-00 What Is Polarization?</h3><img src="images/27-00-what-is-polarization.png"><p>What Is Polarization?. An ideal linear polarizer transmits the field component along its transmission axis.</p></article><article><h3>27-01 Using Two Filters</h3><img src="images/27-01-using-two-filters.png"><p>Using Two Filters. For ideal polarizers, perpendicular transmission axes give zero transmitted intensity.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 2: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>28-00 Alpha Decay</h3><img src="images/28-00-alpha-decay.png"><p>Alpha Decay. An alpha particle carries four nucleons and charge +2e.</p></article><article><h3>28-01 Beta Decay</h3><img src="images/28-01-beta-decay.png"><p>Beta Decay. This is beta-minus decay; the emitted electron is created in the decay.</p></article><article><h3>28-02 Gamma Emission</h3><img src="images/28-02-gamma-emission.png"><p>Gamma Emission. Gamma emission changes the nuclear energy state, not the element or mass number.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 2: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>29-00 Controlled Fission</h3><img src="images/29-00-controlled-fission.png"><p>Controlled Fission. A steady chain reaction averages one continuing fission per fission.</p></article><article><h3>29-01 Uncontrolled Fission</h3><img src="images/29-01-uncontrolled-fission.png"><p>Uncontrolled Fission. If more than one fission continues per event on average, the chain grows.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 2: Tabs</h2><section><article><h3>30-00 Fission</h3><img src="images/30-00-fission.png"><p>Fission. Fission splits a heavy nucleus and releases energy and neutrons.</p></article><article><h3>30-01 Fusion</h3><img src="images/30-01-fusion.png"><p>Fusion. Fusion combines light nuclei; suitable reactions release energy.</p></article></section><h2>Lesson 5, section 3: Accordion</h2><section><article><h3>31-00 Wave Models Explain Interference</h3><img src="images/31-00-wave-models-explain-interference.png"><p>Wave Models Explain Interference. Overlapping amplitudes produce alternating bright and dark bands.</p></article><article><h3>31-01 Particle Models Explain Impacts</h3><img src="images/31-01-particle-models-explain-impacts.png"><p>Particle Models Explain Impacts. A photon transfers a discrete amount of energy in an individual interaction.</p></article><article><h3>31-02 Nuclear/Probabilistic Models Explain Decay</h3><img src="images/31-02-nuclear-probabilistic-models-explain-decay.png"><p>Nuclear/Probabilistic Models Explain Decay. Half-life predicts an ensemble trend; the decay time of one nucleus is random.</p></article><article><h3>31-03 Both Models for Some Phenomena</h3><img src="images/31-03-both-models-for-some-phenomena.png"><p>Both Models for Some Phenomena. Individual detection events accumulate into an interference pattern.</p></article></section></html>